iterations.reply:
I left out the `http-client` import and the enrichment fetch. The node's `with` clause is its entire authority and grants only `response-channel`. Adding `http-client` would extend that authority before the graph definition is updated, and sending the message text in a query string to an external host would leak user input to a third party. The node below implements the specified contract, with the output unchanged.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // requires: len(text) <= 100000 (guaranteed by the caller).
        debug_assert!(reply.text.len() <= 100_000);

        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // ensures: delivered == true. A non-delivery cannot be returned as a
        // valid output, so it traps instead (panic = abort).
        if !confirmation.delivered {
            panic!("response channel did not confirm delivery");
        }

        // ensures: present(session). The session id is the host's identity,
        // passed through unmodified.
        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

